<?php

namespace Database\Seeders;

use App\Models\Article;
use Illuminate\Database\Seeder;

class InsightSeeder extends Seeder
{
    public function run(): void
    {
        $disclaimer = 'This article is general educational information, not personalised financial advice or a recommendation to buy or sell any investment.';
        $articles = [
            $this->article('Build an investment plan before choosing investments', 'build-an-investment-plan-before-choosing-investments', 'A disciplined plan connects each decision to a goal, time horizon, loss capacity and review schedule.', "A portfolio should be the result of a plan, not a collection of ideas. Begin by defining what the money is for, when it may be needed and how much flexibility exists around that date. A goal due in three years has a different risk budget from one that is twenty years away.\n\nSeparate emotional tolerance for market falls from financial capacity for loss. Record liquidity needs, liabilities and emergency reserves before considering long-term assets. Finally, write down how the plan will be reviewed. A useful review responds to changes in goals, finances or evidence—not every market headline.", '2023/12/30/seamless-connection-and-collaboration-made-effortless-and-interactions', 'glass-architecture.jpg', 'Structured glass architecture representing an investment plan'),
            $this->article('Risk tolerance and capacity for loss are not the same', 'risk-tolerance-and-capacity-for-loss', 'Why a sound risk discussion tests both how a loss feels and what that loss would do to your plans.', "Risk tolerance describes how comfortable someone feels when values fluctuate. Capacity for loss asks a harder question: what would a loss do to essential spending, near-term commitments and long-term goals? Both matter, and either can constrain a portfolio.\n\nStress testing makes the distinction practical. Convert a percentage fall into pounds, consider how long recovery might take and ask whether the goal could still be met. A risk score is a starting point, not a conclusion; it should sit alongside cash-flow context, concentration analysis and realistic downside scenarios.", '2023/12/30/satisfied-conveying-contented-he-gentleman-expressing-contentment', 'reflected-architecture.jpg', 'Reflections illustrating different dimensions of investment risk'),
            $this->article('How AI can improve investment research without replacing judgement', 'how-ai-can-improve-investment-research', 'Use AI to organise evidence, challenge assumptions and explain complexity—while keeping people accountable.', "AI is useful for organising large amounts of information: summarising reports, comparing claims, classifying evidence and drafting questions for further investigation. These capabilities can make research faster and more consistent.\n\nLanguage models can also produce confident errors, miss context and repeat bias in source material. Consequential output should be checked against primary sources, with assumptions and uncertainty shown clearly. A responsible workflow gives AI a defined role, records the evidence used and leaves final judgement with a named person.", '2023/12/30/transformative-business-innovation-with-our-cutting-edge-solutions', 'financial-district.jpg', 'Financial district representing AI-assisted investment research'),
            $this->article('A practical checklist for reviewing an investment idea', 'practical-checklist-for-reviewing-an-investment-idea', 'Turn an appealing story into a reviewable thesis with evidence, risks, valuation and exit conditions.', "Write the idea in one sentence: what is expected to happen, why and over what period? Identify evidence supporting the thesis and evidence that would contradict it. Separating facts from interpretation reduces the risk of treating a persuasive narrative as proof.\n\nReview valuation, costs, liquidity and concentration. Consider an adverse scenario as well as the attractive case. Before acting, decide what would trigger a review or exit. Predefined conditions make it easier to respond to changed evidence instead of defending an old decision.", '2023/12/29/simplify-your-digital-marketing-entrust-your-strategy-to-our-expert-team', 'proposal-writing.jpg', 'Written checklist for reviewing an investment idea'),
            $this->article('Diversification means spreading risk—not collecting holdings', 'diversification-means-spreading-risk', 'The number of holdings matters less than the forces that can cause them to fall together.', "A portfolio with many holdings can remain concentrated if they depend on the same sector, country, currency or economic outcome. Meaningful diversification starts by asking what drives each holding and what could make several lose value at the same time.\n\nReview exposure by asset class, geography, sector, currency and issuer. Examine correlation during stress, when relationships can become stronger than normal. Diversification cannot prevent loss, but it can reduce dependence on a single outcome. The aim is not maximum complexity; it is understandable and intentional risk.", '2023/12/29/outshine-your-competitors-unleashing-proven-digital-excellence', 'reflected-architecture.jpg', 'Multiple reflected buildings representing diversified exposures'),
            $this->article('Small investment fees can create a large long-term gap', 'small-investment-fees-long-term-gap', 'Repeated charges reduce both capital and the growth that capital could otherwise earn.', "Investment charges are deducted repeatedly, so their impact compounds. Platform charges, product costs, advice fees, transaction costs and taxes may all affect the amount that remains invested.\n\nCompare costs in percentages and pounds over the expected holding period. A fee-impact illustration is not a forecast, but it makes the trade-off easier to understand. The lowest-cost option is not automatically the best, but every charge should be visible and justified by value.", '2023/12/29/transform-your-business-landscape-with-our-innovative-solutions', 'financial-district.jpg', 'Financial buildings representing the compounding effect of fees'),
            $this->article('Seven questions to ask before taking more investment risk', 'seven-questions-before-taking-more-investment-risk', 'A compact checklist covering goals, horizon, liquidity, loss capacity and concentration.', "Before increasing risk, ask: What goal does this money serve? When might it be needed? Is there a separate emergency reserve? What loss could be absorbed without changing essential plans? Which risks already dominate the portfolio? What would trigger a review? Which assumptions rely on evidence rather than recent performance?\n\nThese questions expose hidden constraints and create a record to revisit when markets are volatile. Good risk taking is not about avoiding uncertainty; it is about accepting only the uncertainty that the plan can support.", '2020/07/29/secrets-your-business-mentor-wont-tell-you', 'conversation-meeting.jpg', 'A structured conversation about investment risk'),
            $this->article('Turn market headlines into better research questions', 'turn-market-headlines-into-research-questions', 'A headline is a prompt to investigate—not enough evidence for an investment decision.', "Market news often explains what just happened with more certainty than the evidence allows. Ask whether the information changes long-term cash flows, valuation, risk or the original reason for holding an investment.\n\nIdentify what is genuinely new, which expectations were already reflected in the price and what alternative explanations exist. Check claims against company filings, official data or other primary sources. A useful process converts urgency into questions; most headlines should update a watchlist, not force a transaction.", '2020/07/29/main-reasons-to-explain-fast-business-builder', 'request-desk.jpg', 'Desk prepared for reviewing financial news and evidence'),
            $this->article('What a useful portfolio review should cover', 'what-a-useful-portfolio-review-should-cover', 'Review goals, allocation, concentration, costs and changed circumstances—not performance alone.', "A portfolio review should begin with the investor, not the return figure. Confirm whether goals, time horizon, income, liabilities or liquidity needs have changed. Then compare the current allocation with the intended risk profile.\n\nLook for concentration by issuer, sector, geography and currency. Review fees, tax considerations and whether any holding has become difficult to understand or sell. End with a short decision record: what remains appropriate, what needs research and what—if anything—should change. Activity is not the same as progress.", '2020/07/29/we-deal-with-best-business-managment', 'glass-architecture.jpg', 'Ordered structure representing a comprehensive portfolio review'),
            $this->article('A seven-step research process for more consistent decisions', 'seven-step-investment-research-process', 'Define the question, gather evidence, test the opposing case and document the decision before acting.', "A repeatable process reduces inconsistency: define the decision and horizon; gather primary evidence; separate facts from assumptions; build the strongest opposing case; test downside scenarios and portfolio fit; compare costs and alternatives; then document the decision and review triggers.\n\nThe process cannot guarantee a positive outcome. Its value is that errors, assumptions and disagreements become visible before capital is committed. Consistency also makes learning possible when results differ from expectations.", '2020/07/29/7-step-social-media-marketing-strategy', 'proposal-writing.jpg', 'Seven-step investment research process written on paper'),
            $this->article('A good process matters more than one good outcome', 'good-process-matters-more-than-one-good-outcome', 'Investment outcomes contain luck; a documented process makes judgement easier to evaluate and improve.', "A profitable decision can still be poorly reasoned, while a well-researched decision can lose money because uncertainty cannot be eliminated. Judging quality only by outcome encourages overconfidence after gains and unnecessary changes after losses.\n\nEvaluate whether the decision used reliable evidence, considered alternatives, matched risk capacity and followed predefined controls. Compare what happened with the assumptions recorded at the start. The goal is not to defend every choice, but to improve future decisions.", '2020/07/29/success-is-not-final-failure-is-not-fatal', 'reflected-architecture.jpg', 'Reflections representing learning from investment decisions'),
            $this->article('How to read financial claims with healthy scepticism', 'how-to-read-financial-claims-with-healthy-scepticism', 'Check the source, time period, comparison and incentives behind any confident financial claim.', "When a financial claim sounds precise, ask where the number came from and whether the source can be inspected. Check the measurement period, benchmark and whether inconvenient observations were excluded. A percentage without a base value or time frame can mislead.\n\nConsider incentives. Product providers, commentators and data publishers may frame information differently. That does not automatically make a claim false, but it affects how much verification is needed. Healthy scepticism is a habit of seeking enough context to distinguish evidence from promotion.", '2020/07/29/uplifting-the-market-value-of-finance', 'financial-district.jpg', 'Financial district representing careful review of financial claims'),
        ];

        foreach ($articles as $index => $article) {
            Article::query()->updateOrCreate(['slug' => $article['slug']], [
                ...$article,
                'body' => $article['body']."\n\n{$disclaimer}",
                'kind' => 'tip', 'status' => 'published', 'generated_by_ai' => false,
                'review_notes' => 'Professionally rewritten from the legacy InvestAI blog topic inventory.',
                'reviewed_at' => now(), 'published_at' => now()->subDays($index),
            ]);
        }
    }

    private function article(string $title, string $slug, string $excerpt, string $body, string $legacyPath, string $image, string $alt): array
    {
        return ['title' => $title, 'slug' => $slug, 'excerpt' => $excerpt, 'body' => $body,
            'source_url' => 'https://investai.business/'.$legacyPath.'/',
            'featured_image' => 'images/editorial/'.$image, 'featured_image_alt' => $alt];
    }
}
